<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Orontes River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Orontes is a river of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, flowing north through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. The city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Kadesh</a> stood on its banks. The Battle of Kadesh, fought there in 1274 BCE between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, was one of the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> battles in history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/orontes-river.jpg" alt="Orontes River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Noria_in_Hama_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ebla lay at Tell Mardikh, about 55 kilometres south-west of Aleppo in northern Syria, on the rolling plain between the <b>Orontes</b> valley and the Euphrates.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ebla">Ebla · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Battle of Kadesh in 1274 BCE, fought against Ramesses II on the <b>Orontes River</b> under Muwatalli II, is often described as the largest chariot engagement of antiquity, with perhaps five thousand chariots involved on both sides.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1178 Saladin granted Hama on the <b>Orontes</b> to his nephew Taqi al-Din Umar, whose descendants kept it as a hereditary principality within the Ayyubid family confederation.</blockquote><a href="/state/ayyubid-emirate-of-hama">Ayyubid Emirate of Hama</a></li><li><blockquote>The county ran along the coast from Jubail to Tortosa and inland to the <b>Orontes</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-tripoli">County of Tripoli</a></li><li><blockquote>Sampsigeramus I, an Arab chief, controlled the <b>Orontes</b> valley around Emesa, modern Homs, as the Seleucid Empire collapsed.</blockquote><a href="/state/emesene-dynasty">Emesene dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Hamath, modern Hama, rose after the Late Bronze Age collapse as a kingdom on the middle <b>Orontes</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/hamath">Hamath</a></li></ul></section>
